"""
Reads the two encoders of the physical QUBE once per tick and places the
measured angles on the clock of the partition it is connected to.

`hw_measure` samples both encoders and caches them; `hw_shoulder` and `hw_elbow`
read that cache back. All three are calls out to C (`csrc/qube_hw.c`, via the
operators in `src/hardware_io.jl`), which is what lets this component compile for
the Julia backend, the C backend and `export_c` alike. The device state lives in
that C library, so nothing is passed through the Dyad interface.

`trig` carries no information: it exists so the two cache reads depend on the
measurement and are therefore scheduled after it. `sampletimesource` supplies the
argument that keeps `hw_measure` in the equations instead of being evaluated while
the model is built -- the same shape as `DiscreteComponents.UniformNoise`, which
calls `seeded_rand(seed, td)`.

Clock-agnostic.
"""
component HardwareMeasurement@[input clk extends Discrete]
  "Measured arm (shoulder) angle, 0 at home"
  shoulder_angle = Dyad.RealOutput@[clk]() {^shoulder_angle}
  "Measured pendulum (elbow) angle, 0 hanging down"
  elbow_angle = Dyad.RealOutput@[clk]() {^elbow_angle}
  sampletimesource = DiscreteComponents.SampleTimeSource() {^sampletimesource}
  "Dependency token: the value of the encoder read, used only to order the reads below"
  variable trig::Real
relations
  trig@clk = hw_measure(sampletimesource.y@clk)
  shoulder_angle@clk = hw_shoulder(trig)
  elbow_angle@clk = hw_elbow(trig)
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://QuanserComponents/HardwareMeasurement.svg"}},
  "_links": {
    "shoulder_angle": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 1000, "x2": 1100, "y1": 170, "y2": 270}}}
    },
    "elbow_angle": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 1000, "x2": 1100, "y1": 720, "y2": 820}}}
    },
    "sampletimesource": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 20, "x2": 120, "y1": 450, "y2": 550}}}
    }
  }
}
end

"""
Writes the commanded motor voltage to the physical QUBE once per tick and
reports back what was actually applied.

`hw_write` clamps the command to `[-umax, umax]`, applies it and returns what was
applied, so `u_applied` may differ from `u`. Like `HardwareMeasurement` the call
goes out to `csrc/qube_hw.c` and works on every target.

Clock-agnostic.
"""
component HardwareCommand@[input clk extends Discrete]
  "Commanded motor voltage"
  u = Dyad.RealInput@[clk]() {^u}
  "Voltage actually written to the amplifier"
  u_applied = Dyad.RealOutput@[clk]() {^u_applied}
  "Saturation applied before writing; the QUBE amplifier range is +/-10 V"
  parameter umax::Dyad.Voltage = 10.0
relations
  u_applied@clk = hw_write(u, umax)
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://QuanserComponents/HardwareCommand.svg"}},
  "_links": {
    "u": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": -100, "x2": 0, "y1": 450, "y2": 550}}}
    },
    "u_applied": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 1000, "x2": 1100, "y1": 450, "y2": 550}}}
    }
  }
}
end

"""
Reports what the control loop itself did this tick: elapsed time, achieved period,
read-to-write duration and the raw encoder counts.

These are wall-clock measurements taken inside `csrc/qube_hw.c` (via the operators in
`src/hardware_io.jl`), so they are available on every target the program compiles for. The
point is that the program can log its own timing instead of a surrounding loop having to
write a second file next to the program's — the whole run ends up in one place, whether the
loop is the Julia driver or the exported C harness.

`dep` is a dependency token, like the one inside `HardwareMeasurement`: connect it to a
signal that is only available *after* the motor write, i.e. the applied voltage. `exec` is
latched by the write, so reading it earlier in the tick would report the previous tick's
value. `time` and `dt` are latched by the read and are correct anywhere after it.

`exec` measures read to write, which is everything the node does bar the logging call
itself, so it runs slightly short of the loop-body time an outside timing loop measures.
`dt` is 0 on the first tick, having no predecessor.

The elapsed-time port is `elapsed` and not `time` because `time` is Dyad's continuous-time
variable: an output of that name makes the component's clocked equation a continuous one and
the model fails to compile with `invalid-clock-usage`. The log column is still called `time`.

Clock-agnostic.
"""
component HardwareDiagnostics@[input clk extends Discrete]
  "Dependency token: connect to a signal produced after the motor write"
  dep = Dyad.RealInput@[clk]() {^dep}
  "Seconds since the first read after the device was opened; 0 on the first tick"
  elapsed = Dyad.RealOutput@[clk]() {^elapsed}
  "Achieved period: seconds since the previous tick's read; 0 on the first tick"
  dt = Dyad.RealOutput@[clk]() {^dt}
  "Seconds from this tick's read to this tick's write"
  exec = Dyad.RealOutput@[clk]() {^exec}
  "Raw shoulder encoder count of the most recent read"
  count_shoulder = Dyad.RealOutput@[clk]() {^count_shoulder}
  "Raw elbow encoder count of the most recent read"
  count_elbow = Dyad.RealOutput@[clk]() {^count_elbow}
relations
  elapsed@clk = hw_time(dep)
  dt@clk = hw_dt(dep)
  exec@clk = hw_exec(dep)
  count_shoulder@clk = hw_count_shoulder(dep)
  count_elbow@clk = hw_count_elbow(dep)
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://QuanserComponents/HardwareDiagnostics.svg"}},
  "_links": {
    "dep": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": -100, "x2": 0, "y1": 450, "y2": 550}}}
    },
    "elapsed": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 1000, "x2": 1100, "y1": 100, "y2": 200}}}
    },
    "dt": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 1000, "x2": 1100, "y1": 275, "y2": 375}}}
    },
    "exec": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 1000, "x2": 1100, "y1": 450, "y2": 550}}}
    },
    "count_shoulder": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 1000, "x2": 1100, "y1": 625, "y2": 725}}}
    },
    "count_elbow": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 1000, "x2": 1100, "y1": 800, "y2": 900}}}
    }
  }
}
end

"""
The swing-up controller closed around the physical QUBE, with the hardware I/O
inside the synchronous program.

`FurutaSwingup` is the same controller closed around the simulated
`QubePendulum`; here the plant is replaced by `HardwareMeasurement` and
`HardwareCommand`, which perform the encoder read and the amplifier write
themselves. Compiling this model therefore yields a synchronous program that
needs nothing from its caller but a clock tick -- see
`generate_swingup_controller` and `SwingupController` in `src/codegen.jl`.

The run log is written from inside the program too, by the `DataLogger`, in the
`SWINGUP_LOG_COLUMNS` order: what was measured and applied, plus the loop
diagnostics `HardwareDiagnostics` reports. So every target logs the same file
through the same code — the C harness around the exported program is timing and
nothing else, and a Julia driver adds no logging of its own either. The driver
opens the file, since a filename cannot cross a synchronous node's interface;
`log_file` is the name it must use, and `open_log!` is called with it
automatically by the runners in src/program.jl.
"""
component FurutaHardware
  measurement = HardwareMeasurement() {^measurement}
  control_system = SwingupWithHoming(final umax = umax) {^control_system}
  command = HardwareCommand(final umax = umax) {^command}
  diagnostics = HardwareDiagnostics() {^diagnostics}
  logger = DataLogger(n = SWINGUP_LOG_NCOLS, filename = log_file, header = SWINGUP_LOG_HEADER) {^logger}
  periodicclock = DiscreteComponents.PeriodicClock(dt = Ts) {^periodicclock}
  "Controller sample time"
  structural parameter Ts::Real = 0.005
  "File the log is written to; the driver opens it with this name"
  structural parameter log_file::String = SWINGUP_LOG_FILE
  """
  Motor saturation [V]: the stabilizer saturates its output here and `HardwareCommand`
  clamps to it as well, so no command outside the amplifier's range can be produced or
  written. Runtime-settable, being one of the compiled program's `TuningGains`
  """
  parameter umax::Dyad.Voltage = 10.0
relations
  connect(measurement.shoulder_angle, control_system.shoulder_angle, logger.u[2], periodicclock.y) {^id7}
  connect(measurement.elbow_angle, control_system.elbow_angle, logger.u[3]) {^id8}
  connect(control_system.u, command.u)
  # The applied voltage is the diagnostics' dependency token as well as a logged column:
  # `exec` is latched by the write, so the reads have to be scheduled after it.
  connect(command.u_applied, diagnostics.dep, logger.u[4]) {^id9}
  connect(diagnostics.elapsed, logger.u[1]) {^id10}
  connect(diagnostics.dt, logger.u[5]) {^id11}
  connect(diagnostics.exec, logger.u[6]) {^id12}
  connect(diagnostics.count_shoulder, logger.u[7]) {^id13}
  connect(diagnostics.count_elbow, logger.u[8]) {^id14}
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://QuanserComponents/FurutaHardware.svg"}},
  "_links": {
    "measurement": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 20, "x2": 120, "y1": 136, "y2": 236}}}
    },
    "control_system": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 160, "x2": 260, "y1": 140, "y2": 240}}}
    },
    "command": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 300, "x2": 400, "y1": 140, "y2": 240}}}
    },
    "diagnostics": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 440, "x2": 540, "y1": 140, "y2": 240}}}
    },
    "logger": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 790, "x2": 890, "y1": 140, "y2": 240}}}
    },
    "periodicclock": {"Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 120, "x2": 20, "y1": 10, "y2": 110}}}},
    "id7": {
      "Dyad": {
        "edges": [
          {"S": 1, "M": [], "E": 2},
          {"S": 2, "M": [{"x": 140, "y": 158}], "E": -1},
          {"S": -1, "M": [{"x": 745, "y": 60}, {"x": 745, "y": 190}], "E": 3},
          {"S": 4, "M": [], "E": -1}
        ],
        "junctions": [{"x": 140, "y": 60}],
        "path": {}
      }
    },
    "id8": {
      "Dyad": {
        "edges": [
          {"S": 1, "M": [], "E": 2},
          {
            "S": 2,
            "M": [
              {"x": 140, "y": 213},
              {"x": 140, "y": 320},
              {"x": 745, "y": 320},
              {"x": 745, "y": 190}
            ],
            "E": 3
          }
        ],
        "path": {}
      }
    },
    "id9": {
      "Dyad": {
        "edges": [
          {"S": 1, "M": [], "E": 2},
          {
            "S": 2,
            "M": [
              {"x": 420, "y": 190},
              {"x": 420, "y": 290},
              {"x": 750, "y": 290},
              {"x": 750, "y": 190}
            ],
            "E": 3
          }
        ],
        "path": {}
      }
    },
    "id10": {"Dyad": {"edges": [{"S": 1, "M": [{"x": 570, "y": 155}], "E": 2}], "path": {}}},
    "id11": {
      "Dyad": {"edges": [{"S": 1, "M": [{"x": 570, "y": 172.5}], "E": 2}], "path": {}}
    },
